<p id="S5.p2.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.9.1" class="ltx_text">Here we move the term linear in <math id="S5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to the left, and that the <math id="S5.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> term is dominated by the <math id="S5.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{n+\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{n+\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> term. So, we have from (<a href="#S5.E5" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. Optimal Regularity ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>)</span></p>
