<p id="S7.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset\mathcal{R}^{n+1}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset\mathcal{R}^{n+1}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open set containing our domain. We note that by our normalization of the eigenfunction, <math id="S7.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|u^{\prime}\|_{L^{2}(\mathcal{R}_{k}^{n})}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|u^{\prime}\|_{L^{2}(\mathcal{R}_{k}^{n})}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\epsilon}(\tau_{\epsilon}(x))=u(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\epsilon}(\tau_{\epsilon}(x))=u(x)</annotation></semantics></math> be our competitor, then we can calculate</span></p>
