<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9. </span>Free Boundary Regularity</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we show that the minimizer <math id="S9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> of energy (<a href="#S5.E15" title="In 5. Optimal Regularity ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.15</span></a>) is an almost minimizer of the following Alt–Caffarelli–Friedman functional, then the free boundary regularity theorem from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> applies. Let</p>
<table id="S9.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{J}(u,\Omega)=\int_{\Omega}|y|^{a}|\nabla u|^{2}+\int_{\Omega\cap\{y=0\}}\chi_{\{u\neq 0\}}d\mathcal{H}^{n-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>a</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>u</mi><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">+</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∩</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{J}(u,\Omega)=\int_{\Omega}|y|^{a}|\nabla u|^{2}+\int_{\Omega\cap\{y=0\}}\chi_{\{u\neq 0\}}d\mathcal{H}^{n-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 9.2</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\subset\mathcal{R}^{n+1}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\subset\mathcal{R}^{n+1}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be an open set. Let <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-harmonic in <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega\setminus\{y=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\setminus\{y=0\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=x_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=x_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x,y)=u(x,-y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x,y)=u(x,-y)</annotation></semantics></math>. We say <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is an almost minimizer of (<a href="#S9.E1" title="In 9. Free Boundary Regularity ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>) if there exists <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S9.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{J}(u,B_{r}(x_{0},0))\leq(1+\kappa r^{\alpha})\tilde{J}(v,B_{r}(x_{0},0))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{J}(u,B_{r}(x_{0},0))\leq(1+\kappa r^{\alpha})\tilde{J}(v,B_{r}(x_{0},0))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for any <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in u+H_{0}^{1}(a,B_{r}(x_{0},0))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in u+H_{0}^{1}(a,B_{r}(x_{0},0))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We recall the notation that for a function <math id="S9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{R}^{n+1}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{R}^{n+1}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{\prime}=f|_{\{y=0\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}=f|_{\{y=0\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for a set <math id="S9.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\in\mathcal{R}^{n+1}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\in\mathcal{R}^{n+1}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega^{\prime}=\Omega\cap\{y=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{\prime}=\Omega\cap\{y=0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9.4</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th eigenfunction corresponding to the minimizing eigenvalue <math id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> be its extension, then <math id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is an almost minimizer of (<a href="#S9.E1" title="In 9. Free Boundary Regularity ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Without loss of generality, let <math id="S9.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|u_{k}\|_{L^{2}(\mathcal{R}^{n}_{k})}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|u_{k}\|_{L^{2}(\mathcal{R}^{n}_{k})}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. We note that it is enough to check equation (<a href="#S9.E3" title="In Definition 9.2. ‣ 9. Free Boundary Regularity ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.3</span></a>) for <math id="S9.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S9.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> are local minimizers of <math id="S9.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{J}(u,B_{r}(x_{0},0))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{J}(u,B_{r}(x_{0},0))</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S9.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=u</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S9.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{k}^{n+1}\setminus B_{r}(x_{0},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{k}^{n+1}\setminus B_{r}(x_{0},0)</annotation></semantics></math>. It is known <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> that <math id="S9.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in C^{0,s}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in C^{0,s}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S9.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subset\subset B_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subset\subset B_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S9.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes (<a href="#S5.E15" title="In 5. Optimal Regularity ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.15</span></a>), we have</span></p>
