<p id="S10.p2.5" class="ltx_p">This is the only non-invariant term under translation of <math id="S10.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S10.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> has the same sign on <math id="S10.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x)u(y)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x)u(y)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S10.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases by moving <math id="S10.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> closer, and if <math id="S10.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> has different signs, <math id="S10.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x)u(y)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x)u(y)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S10.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases by moving <math id="S10.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> further.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S10.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is connected, we expect <math id="S10.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> to be a ball by symmetric decreasing rearrangements. If <math id="S10.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cap{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cap{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has more than one disjoint connected components, by Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 8.3. ‣ 8. Separation of the Free Boundary and Density Estimates ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.3</span></a>, <math id="S10.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> has a sign on each connected component. We can choose <math id="S10.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to be any union of the the connected components with the same sign, and <math id="S10.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{2}=A\setminus A_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{2}=A\setminus A_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S10.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S10.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-minimizer, <math id="S10.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be reduced by translating any union of connected components. We believe this is false and one can always reduce the energy in this case. Thus, we expect that a <math id="S10.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-minimizer is a union of balls in different copies of <math id="S10.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 10.9</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S10.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S10.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S10.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-minimizer, and <math id="S10.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding eigenfunction. If <math id="S10.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is simple, then <math id="S10.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{u_{k}|_{{\mathbb{R}}_{i}^{n}}\neq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{k}|_{{\mathbb{R}}_{i}^{n}}\neq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a ball for all <math id="S10.Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that since <math id="S10.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is not sign-changing on each of the balls, then <math id="S10.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> must be the first eigenfunction on each ball. Since <math id="S10.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a minimizing eigenvalue, <math id="S10.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> has to consists of <math id="S10.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint balls of the same size. In particular, it follows that</p>
<table id="S10.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}(A)=\cdots=\lambda_{k}(A)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}(A)=\cdots=\lambda_{k}(A),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which contradicts that <math id="S10.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is simple. Therefore, Conjecture <a href="#S10.Thmthm9" title="Conjecture 10.9. ‣ 10. The Global Configuration and a Toy Problem ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.9</span></a> implies the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corcon">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary of Conjecture 10.10</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S10.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S10.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S10.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-minimizer, then <math id="S10.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is not simple.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 10.11</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S10.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S10.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Corollary of Conjecture <a href="#S10.Thmthm10" title="Corollary of Conjecture 10.10. ‣ 10. The Global Configuration and a Toy Problem ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.10</span></a> is the nonlocal analogy of the conjecture made in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> which states that in the local case, such minimizing <math id="S10.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is not simple.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conjecture <a href="#S10.Thmthm9" title="Conjecture 10.9. ‣ 10. The Global Configuration and a Toy Problem ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.9</span></a> is true if <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2,3</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint connected sets. When there are two disjoint connected sets, if <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> has the same sign on them, <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> decreases by translating them close together, and if <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> has different signs on each of them, <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> decreases by translating them further away in the direction opposite of each other. If <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=3</annotation></semantics></math>, either they all have the same sign, in which case moving them closer together decreases <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, or exactly one of them has a different sign, in which case, moving this one away from the other two decreases the energy. We note that we can always do above translations smoothly when <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2,3</annotation></semantics></math>, and these perturbations keep the order of the eigenvalues.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;3</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint connected sets, let <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A^{+}=\{u&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{+}=\{u&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A^{-}=\{u&lt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{-}=\{u&lt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We note that we can put <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> in different copies of <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, or equivalently, put <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> infinitely away from each other, which reduces the energy in (<a href="#S10.E2" title="In 10. The Global Configuration and a Toy Problem ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>). However, it is not clear that this reduced energy corresponds to the <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-th" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-th</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalue.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As <math id="S10.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\rightarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\rightarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math>, our <math id="S10.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-\Delta)^{s}\varphi=-\Delta\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-\Delta)^{s}\varphi=-\Delta\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S10.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\in C_{0}^{\infty}({\mathbb{R}}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\in C_{0}^{\infty}({\mathbb{R}}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, this nonlocal problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) offers a new perspective in the study of the minimizer of the <math id="S10.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-th" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-th</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalue of <math id="S10.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In (<a href="#S10.E7" title="In 10. The Global Configuration and a Toy Problem ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.7</span></a>), if we replace <math id="S10.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by their enter of mass, we have the following discrete toy problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x_{1},\cdots,x_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x_{1},\cdots,x_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with signed mass <math id="S10.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S10.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{d}|m(x_{i})|^{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{d}|m(x_{i})|^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let the energy of the system be</p>
<table id="S10.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10.12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(m(x))=\sum_{i,j=1}^{d}\frac{-2m(x_{i})m(x_{j})}{|x_{i}-x_{j}|^{n+2s}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(m(x))=\sum_{i,j=1}^{d}\frac{-2m(x_{i})m(x_{j})}{|x_{i}-x_{j}|^{n+2s}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p6.2" class="ltx_p">We say that the configuration <math id="S10.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\cdots,x_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\cdots,x_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is stationary and stable if for every <math id="S10.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=(v_{1},...,v_{d}),v_{i}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(v_{1},...,v_{d}),v_{i}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S10.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10.13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{t\rightarrow 0}\frac{1}{t}(E(x+tv)-E(x))=0,\quad\lim_{t\rightarrow 0}\frac{1}{t^{2}}(E(x+tv)+E(x-tv)-2E(x))\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.889em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{t\rightarrow 0}\frac{1}{t}(E(x+tv)-E(x))=0,\quad\lim_{t\rightarrow 0}\frac{1}{t^{2}}(E(x+tv)+E(x-tv)-2E(x))\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p6.3" class="ltx_p">An associated conjecture to Conjecture <a href="#S10.Thmthm9" title="Conjecture 10.9. ‣ 10. The Global Configuration and a Toy Problem ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.9</span></a> for the toy problem is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 10.14</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<a href="#S10.E12" title="In 10. The Global Configuration and a Toy Problem ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.12</span></a>) doesn’t have any local minimum under for <math id="S10.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 10.15</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S10.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S10.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S10.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The discrete toy problem is a simplified version of the minimization problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>), and understanding this discrete problem would help us in understanding the global configuration of the <math id="S10.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-minimizers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S10.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In (<a href="#S10.E12" title="In 10. The Global Configuration and a Toy Problem ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.12</span></a>), when <math id="S10.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=-1</annotation></semantics></math>, Earnshaw’s theorem states that there is no stable and stationary configurations. However, the proof doesn’t translate to the case <math id="S10.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S10.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S10.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i},x_{j}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i},x_{j}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, let</p>
<table id="S10.EGx78" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S10.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10.16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{dist}\textit{Proj}_{\mathbf{v}}{x_{j}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mtext>dist</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Proj</mtext></mrow><mi>𝐯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{dist}\textit{Proj}_{\mathbf{v}}{x_{j}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S10.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sqrt{|x_{i}-x_{j}|^{2}-d{ij}^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sqrt{|x_{i}-x_{j}|^{2}-d{ij}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S10.I2.i3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I2.i3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S10.I2.i3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x_{1},\cdots,x_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x_{1},\cdots,x_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> with mass <math id="S10.I2.i3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(x_{1}),\cdots,m(x_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(x_{1}),\cdots,m(x_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> achieves a local minimum of <math id="S10.I2.i3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S10.E12" title="In 10. The Global Configuration and a Toy Problem ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.12</span></a>), then (<a href="#S10.E13" title="In 10. The Global Configuration and a Toy Problem ‣ Minimizing Eigenvalues of the Fractional Laplacian" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.13</span></a>) gives the following explicit expression.</p>
<table id="S10.EGx79" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S10.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10.17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{j\neq i}\frac{m(x_{i})m(x_{j})(n+2s)d_{ij}}{\sqrt{r_{ij}^{2}+d_{ij}^{2}}^{n+2s+2}}=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msup><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{j\neq i}\frac{m(x_{i})m(x_{j})(n+2s)d_{ij}}{\sqrt{r_{ij}^{2}+d_{ij}^{2}}^{n+2s+2}}=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S10.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{j\neq i}\frac{m(x_{i})m_{(}x_{j})[r_{ij}^{2}-d_{ij}^{2}(n+2s+1)]}{\sqrt{r_{ij}^{2}+d_{ij}^{2}}^{n+2s+4}}\geq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo></msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{j\neq i}\frac{m(x_{i})m_{(}x_{j})[r_{ij}^{2}-d_{ij}^{2}(n+2s+1)]}{\sqrt{r_{ij}^{2}+d_{ij}^{2}}^{n+2s+4}}\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
